DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     dddd copies its input file to its output file, optionally per-
     forming one or more conversions along the way, with input
     and output block sizes that may differ from each other and
     from the buffering that ccaatt(1) or a shell redirection would
     use.  It is most useful for raw disk or tape devices and for
     exchanging data with EBCDIC systems.

     Every argument has the form _o_p_t_i_o_n= _v_a_l_u_e; there are no
     single-letter flags.  Any argument lacking an = or with
     nothing after the = is an error.  A numeric _v_a_l_u_e may be
     followed by bb, ww, or kk, multiplying it by 512, by the size
     of an iinntt, or by 1024, respectively; two such numbers
     separated by xx are multiplied together.  All buffer sizes
     default to 512 bytes.

     iiff=_f_i_l_e  Read input from _f_i_l_e instead of the standard input.

     ooff=_f_i_l_e  Write output to _f_i_l_e instead of the standard out-
              put.

     iibbss=_n    Set the input buffer size to _n bytes.

     oobbss=_n    Set the output buffer size to _n bytes.

     bbss=_n     Set both the input and the output buffer size to _n
              bytes; if bbss= is the only sizing option given, in-
              put is copied to output without the extra buffer
              that separate iibbss= and oobbss= values require, which
              is faster.

     ccbbss=_n    Set the size of the intermediate conversion buffer
              used by the aasscciiii, eebbccddiicc, and iibbmm conversions to _n
              bytes (default: the input buffer size).

     sskkiipp=_n   Skip _n input buffers (of size iibbss) before copying
              begins.

     sseeeekk=_n   Seek _n output buffers (of size oobbss) into the output
              file before the first write.

     ccoouunntt=_n  Copy at most _n input buffers.

