              aasscciiii    Convert EBCDIC input to ASCII, trimming
                       trailing blanks and appending a newline to
                       each converted record.

              eebbccddiicc   Convert ASCII input to standard (CACM)
                       EBCDIC, blank-padding each record to ccbbss.

              iibbmm      Convert ASCII input to a variant EBCDIC
                       table more suited to IBM line-printer
                       codes, blank-padding each record to ccbbss.

              llccaassee    Map upper-case letters to lower case.

              uuccaassee    Map lower-case letters to upper case.

              sswwaabb     Swap each pair of input bytes before
                       writing.

              ssyynncc     Pad every input record short of iibbss bytes
                       with null bytes.

              nnooeerrrroorr  Continue with the next block after a read
                       or write error instead of stopping.

     aasscciiii and eebbccddiicc, aasscciiii and iibbmm, and uuccaassee and llccaassee may not
     be combined with each other.  Any of the ASCII/EBCDIC con-
     versions forces the use of the intermediate conversion buff-
     er, even if iibbss equals oobbss.

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     When it finishes, dddd prints, on the standard error, the num-
     ber of full and partial records read and the number of full
     and partial records written, e.g.  2200+11 rreeccoorrddss iinn and  2200+00
     rreeccoorrddss oouutt. It exits with status 1 if it was given a bad
     option, an out-of-memory condition, or an unrecovered
     read/write error (one not masked by ccoonnvv=nnooeerrrroorr).

NNOOTTEESS
     Because implementations disagree on the mapping of some
     EBCDIC graphic characters, such as braces and backslash,
     neither the eebbccddiicc nor the iibbmm conversion table is right for
     every application; choose whichever one matches the systems
     being interchanged with.
